hey guys welcome to our angry barrels  making up  we're gonna show you guys all the tips  and tricks take a look and see what you  think  so yeah there's kind of there's kind of  a running joke going uh with niko's  phone because he has this old flip phone  when a new video of ours breaks a  million views we will both get an iphone  4. as of right now i'm stuck with my  crappy flip phone yep and that's kind of  where this whole thing stems from  let's talk about barrels so as you guys  can see this is a really cg heavy piece  uh pretty much everything we interact  with is fake sam's actually cg for the  entire piece so for the barrels  basically i took a pre-existing barrel  model that i had and re-textured it as  you can see here  and for the slingshot what i did is i  made a rough model i kind of smoothed  the edges a little bit and then i  brought it in the mud box to put the  final touches and texture it sam used a  tablet for sculpting the slingshot in  mudbox you guys might have also heard of  a program called zbrush which is  basically the same thing for the effect  of the slingshot firing what i did is i  built a rough model of the sling and  then i used a cloth simulation to drape  it and stretch it what it does is it  gives it that natural elasticity that  you kind of expect from the slingshot  it's much faster than animating it by  hand as well it looks better this is all  done with 3ds max just using the  built-in cloth physics that comes with  the program once i have the slingshot  modeled and animated we uh use boujou to  put it into the scene now we also we get  a lot of questions with people asking  how do you render out something to put  it on footage well the answer is  actually pretty simple basically you  just render it into a format that has  alpha channels alpha channels are  transparency it's computer lingo for  transparency so formats like png  tiff's  exr one key part to making the 3d  objects integrate well with the live  footage is uh by rendering it with three  layers the first being the actual object  sitting by itself nothing else no ground  the second is just the shadows that go  underneath it and yeah and the third is  the ambient occlusion pass that kind of  gives the contact shadows you need once  all three of those are in the scene it  gives you a very realistic looking  composite now for the building  um we only see two sides of the building  so i didn't put a whole lot of detail  into the other side this both saved me  time and also didn't make the physics  simulation as complicated now for the  building really is just a matter of  building one window one pillar one  wrench and then just duplicating it and  moving it across the entire building so  it looked correct now when it came time  to do the physics simulation i  pre-fractured the entire building using  rayfire with everything pre-fractured i  could then have a box activated which is  this big blue box you see here and the  barrels inside this box so as the box  gets into the building it activates all  the geometry the barrel is hitting  everything it's flying all over the  place and it's just physics galore and  then for the actual building collapsing  is essentially the same thing except i  also use the bomb modifier to blow it up  and these big boxes you see here  activate the geometry and have  everything else fall over at the end  after that it's just a matter of  rendering it out  and sticking it on the footage  also thanks to jimmy for being the man  behind the camera  you
 

